$DATUM 108-88-3
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Synthesis of 4-(4-Nitrophenoxy) benzaldehyde (NPBA) NPBA was synthesized
by nucleophilic substitution reaction between 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde and
1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene. This method was based on Bunnett’s theory of
aromatic nucleophilic substitution reactions [30]. In a 250-mL
three-necked round-bottomed flask equipped with a reflux condenser and
mechanical agitator, a mixture of 4-hydroxybenzaldehyde (18.3 g, 0.15
mol), 1-chloro-4-nitrobenzene (23.64 g, 0.15 mol), potassium carbonate
(20.7 g, 0.15 mol), DMF (150 mL), and methylbenzene (40 mL) was added and
fractionated to remove water at 140°C for 6 h. Upon cooling, the mixture
was precipitated into water, and the precipitate was filtered off and
washed thoroughly with water. The crude product was recrystallized from
anhydrous ethanol and then dried at 60 °C under vacuum for 6 h. Yield:
27.48 g (75.39 wt%). FT-IR (KBr): ν = 1512 and 1348 cm-1 (-NO2), 1693,
2757, and 2848 cm-1 (-CHO), 1248 and 1111 cm-1 (-O-). 1H-NMR (DMSO-d6,
300 MHz): δ(ppm) = 10.01 (s, 1H), 8.30-8.32 (d, 2H), 8.00-8.04 (d, 2H),
7.34-7.36 (d, 2H), and 7.29-7.31 (d, 2H).
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM Synthesis of soluble and thermally stable polyimides with phthalimide as
pendent group from pyridine-containing triamine
